        {
          "@id": "urn:uuid:528057ef-fafb-5087-9ca0-1c12d3f3daf9",
          "@type": "uco-observable:ExtractedStringsFacet",
          "uco-observable:strings": [
            {
              "@id": "urn:uuid:b69dd1b9-36db-5a6e-ae48-6a90ee76e071",
              "@type": "uco-observable:ExtractedString",
              "uco-observable:stringValue": "IN THE UNITED STATES DISTRICT COURT\n\nFOR THE DISTRICT OF ALASKA\n\nUNITED STATES OF AMERICA,\n\nPlaintiff, Case No. 3:19-cr-00003-RRB\n\nvs.\nFINDINGS OF FACT,\nTRISTAN GRANT, CONCLUSIONS OF LAW, AND\nVERDICT\nDefendant.\n\nOn December 18, 2018, a Complaint was filed by the United States of\n\nAmerica against Defendant, Tristan Jamal Grant, in the United States District Court for the\n\nDistrict of Alaska. Defendant was arrested on that date, and has been in custody, with one\n\nbrief exception, ever since. After the initial Complaint, several additional pleadings were\n\nfiled, culminating with a Fourth Superseding Indictment filed on November 21, 2019,\n\nwhich is the subject of the instant Court trial. 1\n\nDefendant initially was represented by attorney Scott Dattan, but, after\n\nDefendant expressed concern about his representation, Mr. Dattan was permitted to\n\nwithdraw as counsel on March 31, 2020. 2 On April 3, 2020, attorney Steven Wells\n\n1\nDocket 68.\n2\nDocket 84.\n\nCase 3:19-cr-00003-RRB-MMS Document 214 Filed 04/14/21 Page 1 of 11\nappeared on Defendant\u2019s behalf. 3 Due to a breakdown in the attorney/client relationship,\n\nthe Magistrate Judge permitted Mr. Wells to withdraw as counsel on October 9, 2020. On\n\nOctober 26, 2020, attorney James A. Wendt appeared on Defendant\u2019s behalf, and remains\n\ncounsel of record.\n\nThroughout these proceedings, defense counsel sought continuances of trial\n\nwhile discovery was exchanged and reviewed, negotiations took place, and pandemic\n\nconcerns were addressed. In every instance, the Court found, as required by 18 U.S.C.\n\n\u00a7 3161(h)(7)(A), that the continuance was in the interest of justice, and noted that the\n\ninterest of justice outweighed the interest of Defendant and of the community in proceeding\n\nas scheduled. Moreover, inasmuch as the Court\u2019s Miscellaneous General Orders prohibited\n\njury trials through much of 2020 and well into 2021, the Court indicated that the matter\n\ncould be resolved by a court trial pursuant to Rule 23(a) of the Federal Rules of Criminal\n\nProcedure, if the parties agreed. Defendant readily accepted this offer, as did the\n\nGovernment. After consultation with counsel, Defendant filed a written Waiver of Jury\n\nTrial. 4 On numerous occasions, before and after this waiver was filed, the Court addressed\n\nthe issue with Defendant, who remained adamant that the matter be resolved by a court\n\ntrial. As a result, the Court scheduled trial to proceed beginning February 22, 2021. In\n\norder to ensure that Defendant had adequate access to his counsel and discovery prior to\n\ntrial, the Magistrate Judge permitted Defendant\u2019s release from custody, in the company of\n\n3\nDocket 88.\n4\nDocket 139.\n\nUnited States v. Grant Case No. 3:19-cr-00003-RRB\nFindings of Fact and Conclusions of Law Page 2\nCase 3:19-cr-00003-RRB-MMS Document 214 Filed 04/14/21 Page 2 of 11\nhis attorney\u2019s investigator, to spend time at his attorney\u2019s office. 5 Defendant, however,\n\nabsconded from custody on February 16, 2021. His whereabouts were unknown until his\n\ncapture and return to custody on February 17, 2021. This necessitated a continuance of the\n\ntrial date. Defendant, however, remained anxious to proceed to trial by court at the parties\u2019\n\nearliest convenience.\n\nThe Government was concerned, though, that it might call the defense\n\ninvestigator to testify at trial, and suggested this could create a conflict for Defendant\u2019s\n\nattorney. To address this, the Court appointed attorney Samuel J. Fortier to meet with\n\nDefendant and address all the issues related to this conflict in order to determine whether\n\nDefendant knowingly wanted to waive any such conflict and still desired to proceed to trial\n\nwith his attorney. A detailed waiver was filed by Defendant, indicating that he waived any\n\nconflict that might be generated if his attorney\u2019s investigator was called to testify at trial. 6\n\nDefendant confirmed this orally on March 2, 2021. 7 With all this completed to the parties\u2019\n\nsatisfaction, it was agreed that the matter would proceed to a court trial beginning April 12,\n\n2021.\n\nOn the eve of trial, Defendant filed a Motion for Dismissal with Prejudice, 8\n\nwhich is not yet ripe and which will be addressed in due course.\n\nOn the morning of trial, Defendant once again requested that the trial be\n\ncontinued so that Defendant could further review discovery and consider additional\n\n5\nDockets 168, 167.\n6\nDocket 183.\n7\nDocket 181.\n8\nDocket 209.\n\nUnited States v. Grant Case No. 3:19-cr-00003-RRB\nFindings of Fact and Conclusions of Law Page 3\nCase 3:19-cr-00003-RRB-MMS Document 214 Filed 04/14/21 Page 3 of 11\nwitnesses. The Court denied this motion, holding, for the reasons set forth by the\n\nGovernment and because Defendant had ample opportunity to review discovery in the past,\n\nthat the motion was not in the interest of justice. The Court noted, that shortly before trial\n\nDefendant was released from custody to meet with his attorney for this very reason, but\n\nabsconded. In every instance, Defendant failed to take full advantage of the opportunities\n\nprovided, which led the Court to conclude that Defendant was attempting to manipulate\n\nthe system.\n\nFOURTH SUPERSEDING INDICTMENT\n\nDefendant is charged with ten Counts of criminal activity in the Fourth\n\nSuperseding Indictment, and two additional Counts of Criminal Forfeiture. 9 Defendant\n\ndenies every element of each of these Counts.\n\nELEMENTS AND DEFINITION OF TERMS\n\nThe Government filed its proposed Elements of the Charged Offenses, which\n\nthe Court has reviewed and hereby ADOPTS. 10 Contained therein are the definitions of\n\nrelevant terms, which were not disputed by the parties and which the Court further\n\nADOPTS, but will not set forth herein.\n\nTRIAL\n\nTrial began as scheduled on April 12, 2021, and lasted three days. Social\n\ndistancing guidelines were followed without incident, and the proceedings remained open\n\nto the public.\n\n9\nDocket 68.\n10\nDocket 162.\n\nUnited States v. Grant Case No. 3:19-cr-00003-RRB\nFindings of Fact and Conclusions of Law Page 4\nCase 3:19-cr-00003-RRB-MMS Document 214 Filed 04/14/21 Page 4 of 11\nThe Government called ten witnesses, most of whom were cross-examined\n\nby Defense counsel. Defendant chose not to call any witnesses or testify himself.\n\nSTIPULATED FACTS\n\nThe parties stipulated to nine facts. 11 The stipulation addressed the interstate\n\nnexus of various items seized in this matter, as well as the fact that Defendant previously\n\nhad been convicted of a crime punishable for a term exceeding one year. The Court accepts\n\nthese stipulations and ADOPTS them for purposes of the final verdict in this matter.\n\nFINDINGS OF FACT AND CONCLUSIONS OF LAW\n\nThe evidence presented at trial\u2014testimonial, documentary, and video\u2014\n\noverwhelmingly established, beyond a reasonable doubt, each of the elements of each of\n\nthe Counts set forth by the Government in the Fourth Superseding Indictment.\n\nDefendant, however, challenged the credibility of Ms. Banks, and K.C., and\n\nJ.S., and argued that he did not know and reasonably could not have learned that J.S. and\n\nK.C. were under the age of 18 years at the times in question. Defendant, therefore,\n\ncontends that he should not be guilty of Counts 1 through 8. Defendant\u2019s argument,\n\nhowever, was not credible. Ms. Banks testified that she discussed the minor status of these\n\ntwo persons with Defendant, and both J.S. and K.C. testified credibly that they told\n\nDefendant their ages. The age of the minors was alluded to frequently in text messages\n\nbetween Defendant and Ms. Banks, as well as in casual discussion among the parties.\n\nMoreover, the Court\u2019s observation of K.C. at the time of trial, who was 15 years old at the\n\n11\nDocket 211.\n\nUnited States v. Grant Case No. 3:19-cr-00003-RRB\nFindings of Fact and Conclusions of Law Page 5\nCase 3:19-cr-00003-RRB-MMS Document 214 Filed 04/14/21 Page 5 of 11\ntime she interacted with Defendant and 17 at the time of her trial testimony, revealed a very\n\nyoung-looking girl. J.S. also was clear that Defendant knew her age and was aware that\n\nJ.S. previously had been housed in McLaughlin Youth Center in Anchorage, which was\n\nknown to house juveniles. Defendant, himself, admitted in his post arrest interview with\n\nAgent Torres that both J.S. and K.C. looked young, and would perform \u201ctricks\u201d on occasion\n\nwhere they would tell a prospective client that they were minors and extort payment from\n\nthe client without providing services. Throughout Defendant\u2019s relationship with Banks\n\nand J.S. and K.C., all of whom were vulnerable and very needy, Defendant exercised\n\nsignificant control, benefitting from their commercial sexual encounters and videotaping\n\nMs. Banks and J.S. in sexually explicit acts in the hope of profiting therefrom. Therefore,\n\nDefendant failed to prove a \u201cmistake of age\u201d defense by clear and convincing evidence.\n\nWith regard to Counts 9 and 10 of the Fourth Superseding Indictment, the\n\nvideo evidence produced at trial clearly depicted Defendant in possession of the firearms\n\nand ammunition as charged, and corroborated the testimony of witnesses Banks and K.C.,\n\nwho testified as to their observation of Defendant with these firearms. Defendant admitted\n\nto possession of the Glock in his post-conviction interview with Agent Torres, and admitted\n\nopening and moving the box containing the Nano pistol, which was purchased with\n\nDefendant\u2019s money. Defendant also admitted that he was a felon. Moreover, the firearms\n\nand ammunition in question were found in the vehicle in which Defendant was a passenger\n\nat the time of his arrest.\n\nUnited States v. Grant Case No. 3:19-cr-00003-RRB\nFindings of Fact and Conclusions of Law Page 6\nCase 3:19-cr-00003-RRB-MMS Document 214 Filed 04/14/21 Page 6 of 11\nCOUNT 1\nConspiracy to Engage in Sex Trafficking of Minors\n18 U.S.C. \u00a7 1594(c)\n\nThe evidence presented at trial established beyond a reasonable doubt that,\n\nduring the timeframe alleged in the Fourth Superseding Indictment, Defendant entered into\n\nan agreement with Ajela Banks, an adult female, to engage in the sex trafficking of minors,\n\nto wit: J.S. and K.C., knowing the ages of the minors, knowing the illegal purpose of the\n\nconspiracy, intending to help accomplish that purpose, and acting throughout consistent\n\nwith that purpose as charged in Count 1 of the Fourth Superseding Indictment.\n\nCOUNTS 2 AND 4\nSex Trafficking of a Minor\n18 U.S.C. \u00a7 1591(a)(1)\n\nIt was further established beyond a reasonable doubt that during the dates\n\nalleged in the Fourth Superseding Indictment, Defendant, in concert with Ms. Banks,\n\nknowingly recruited, enticed, harbored, transported, provided, obtained, or maintained\n\npersons, J.S. in Count 2 and K.C. in Count 4; knowing that the aforesaid persons had not\n\nattained the age of 18 years; knowing that the aforesaid minors would be caused to engage\n\nin commercial sex acts; and affecting interstate commerce by utilizing means of interstate\n\ncommerce to communicate, transact, and advertise the illegal activity, as alleged in Counts\n\n2 and 4 of the Fourth Superseding Indictment.\n\nCOUNTS 3 AND 5\nParticipating in a Sex Trafficking Venture\n18 U.S.C. \u00a7 1591(a)(2)\n\nIt was further established beyond a reasonable doubt that during the dates\n\nalleged in the Fourth Superseding Indictment, Defendant engaged in a Sex Trafficking\n\nUnited States v. Grant Case No. 3:19-cr-00003-RRB\nFindings of Fact and Conclusions of Law Page 7\nCase 3:19-cr-00003-RRB-MMS Document 214 Filed 04/14/21 Page 7 of 11\nVenture, in that Defendant knowingly benefitted financially and by receiving other items\n\nof value when Defendant participated in a venture that included recruitment, enticement,\n\nharboring, transporting, obtaining, and maintaining of persons, to wit: J.S. in Count 3 and\n\nK.C. in Count 5; knowing that the aforesaid persons had not attained the age of 18 years;\n\nknowing that the aforesaid minors would be caused to engage in commercial sex acts; and\n\nutilizing and affecting interstate commerce, by transacting, communicating, and\n\nadvertising the sex trafficking venture, as alleged in Counts 3 and 5 of the Fourth\n\nSuperseding Indictment.\n\nCOUNTS 6, 7, AND 8\nSexual Exploitation of a Child\u2014Production of Child Pornography\n18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2251(a), (e)\n\nIt was further established beyond a reasonable doubt that during the dates\n\nalleged in the Fourth Superseding Indictment, J.S. was under the age of 18 years; that\n\nDefendant engaged in the Sexual Exploitation of a Child, Production of Child Pornography\n\non three separate occasions\u2014November 26, 2018, for Count 6, November 27, 2018, for\n\nCount 7, and beginning in at a time unknown but ending on December 12, 2018, for Count\n\n8; that Defendant engaged, employed, used, persuaded and coerced J.S. to take part in\n\nsexually explicit conduct for the purpose of producing a sexual depiction of such conduct;\n\nthat Defendant knew, and had reason to know, that the visual depiction would be mailed,\n\ntransmitted, or transported across state lines or in foreign commerce; that the visual\n\ndepiction was produced using materials that had been mailed, shipped or transported across\n\nstate lines or in foreign commerce; and that the visual depiction was mailed, transmitted,\n\nUnited States v. Grant Case No. 3:19-cr-00003-RRB\nFindings of Fact and Conclusions of Law Page 8\nCase 3:19-cr-00003-RRB-MMS Document 214 Filed 04/14/21 Page 8 of 11\nor actually transported in state or foreign commerce, as alleged in Counts 6, 7, and 8 of the\n\nFourth Superseding Indictment.\n\nCOUNT 9\nFelon in Possession of Firearm and Ammunition\n18 USC 922(g)(1) and 924(a)(2)\n\nIt was further established, beyond a reasonable doubt, that between on or\n\nabout November 15, 2018, and on or about December 13, 2018, Defendant knowingly\n\npossessed a Glock 10mm caliber pistol, model 29GEN4, and 12 rounds of 10mm\n\nammunition; that at the time Defendant possessed the aforesaid pistol and ammunition,\n\nDefendant had been convicted of a crime punishable by imprisonment for a term exceeding\n\none year; that at the time Defendant possessed the Glock 10mm caliber pistol and the 10mm\n\nammunition, Defendant knew that he had been convicted of a crime punishable by\n\nimprisonment for a term exceeding one year; and that the aforesaid pistol and ammunition\n\nhad been shipped or transported from on state to another, or between a foreign nation and\n\nthe United States, as alleged in Count 9 of the Fourth Superseding Indictment.\n\nCOUNT 10\nFelon in Possession of Firearm and Ammunition\n18 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7 922(g)(1) and 924(a)(2)\n\nIt was further established, beyond a reasonable doubt, that between on or\n\nabout December 7, 2018, and on or about December 13, 2018, Defendant knowingly\n\npossessed a AIO Inc 7.62x39 caliber M214 Nano pistol and 20 rounds of 7.62x39\n\nammunition; that at the time Defendant possessed the aforesaid pistol and ammunition\n\nDefendant had been convicted of a crime punishable by imprisonment for a term exceeding\n\none year; that at the time Defendant possessed the aforesaid pistol and ammunition,\n\nUnited States v. Grant Case No. 3:19-cr-00003-RRB\nFindings of Fact and Conclusions of Law Page 9\nCase 3:19-cr-00003-RRB-MMS Document 214 Filed 04/14/21 Page 9 of 11\nDefendant knew that he had been convicted of a crime punishable by imprisonment for a\n\nterm exceeding one year; and that the aforesaid pistol and ammunition had been shipped\n\nfrom one a state to another, or between a foreign nation and the United States, as alleged\n\nin Count 10 of the Fourth Superseding Indictment.\n\nVERDICT\n\nFor the reasons set forth above, as well as for those reasons set forth by the\n\nGovernment in its Trial Brief, 12 the Court hereby finds Defendant, Tristan Jamal Grant,\n\nGUILTY OF COUNTS 1 THROUGH 10 of the Fourth Superseding Indictment, the\n\nGovernment having established each element of each Count beyond a reasonable doubt.\n\nFORFEITURE\n\nDefendant, having been found Guilty of Counts 6, 7, 8, 9, and 10 of the\n\nFourth Superseding Indictment, the Court hereby finds Defendant GUILTY of the\n\nForfeiture allegations set forth in the Fourth Superseding Indictment. Defendant, therefore,\n\nshall forfeit to the United States of America, pursuant to 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 924(d) and 28 U.S.C.\n\n\u00a7 2461(c), the following property as set forth in Criminal Forfeiture Allegation 1: any\n\nfirearm or ammunition involved in or used in knowing violation of the offense, including,\n\nbut not limited to the following:\n\n1. A Glock 10mm caliber pistol, Model 29GEN4, serial\n\nnumber BCNA440;\n\n2. 12 rounds of 10mm ammunition;\n\n12\nDocket 161.\n\nUnited States v. Grant Case No. 3:19-cr-00003-RRB\nFindings of Fact and Conclusions of Law Page 10\nCase 3:19-cr-00003-RRB-MMS Document 214 Filed 04/14/21 Page 10 of 11\n3. An IO Inc., 7.62x39 caliber M214 Nano pistol, serial\n\nnumber SO34347; and\n\n4. 20 rounds of 7.62x39 ammunition.\n\nAll pursuant to 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 924(d) and 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 2461(c), and Rule 32.2(a) of the Federal\n\nRules of Criminal Procedure.\n\nDefendant also shall forfeit to the United States pursuant to 18 U.S.C. \u00a7 2253\n\nany property, real or personal, that was used or intended to be used to commit or to facilitate\n\nthe commission or any of the aforesaid violations, and any property, real or personal,\n\nconstituting or derived from any proceeds obtained, directly or indirectly, as a result of\n\nsuch violations as set forth in Criminal Forfeiture allegation 2, all pursuant to 18 U.S.C.\n\n\u00a7 2253 and 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 2461(c), and Rule 32.2(a) of the Federal Rules of Criminal\n\nProcedure.\n\nJUDGMENT\n\nA Judgment shall enter once the Motion for Dismissal with Prejudice 13 is\n\nresolved, and, if necessary, once sentence has been imposed.\n\nIT IS SO ORDERED this 14th day of April, 2021, at Anchorage, Alaska.\n\n/s/ Ralph R. Beistline\nRALPH R. BEISTLINE\nSenior United States District Judge\n\n13\nDocket 209.\n\nUnited States v. Grant Case No. 3:19-cr-00003-RRB\nFindings of Fact and Conclusions of Law Page 11\nCase 3:19-cr-00003-RRB-MMS Document 214 Filed 04/14/21 Page 11 of 11"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
